 "report_paragraph_draft": "Time as a fourth variable (causal test; results/p5_time_predictor.json, positional control results/p5_time_positional.json). The within-clip time code looks like a variable: it is a shared curve holding 0.77-0.86 of each clip's within-clip variance, pointing the same way in every clip (mean pairwise cosine of per-clip time directions 0.73 at point 12, vs 0.05 in the untrained copy, whose own time signal is 0.7% of within-clip variance), the same way for slow and fast clips (band cosine 0.96), and orthogonal to the speed and direction readouts (|cos| 0.015 and 0.043, below the 0.07 clip-difference null). Steering it within the encoder lands on the target step (0.10 steps error vs 0.55-0.61 in the untrained copy). But the predictor does not read it as elapsed time. Adding the spline's +2-step displacement to every context token at point 22 moves the forecast disk -3.7 px [-5.8, -1.6] along its trajectory (advance -0.21 [-0.42, -0.01] of the true two-step displacement), no differently from the same path aimed backwards (gap +0.02 px [-2.0, 1.8]), while pushing it 14.1 px sideways and changing forecast speed by +2.0 m/s. At point 12 the forecast advances 0.19 [0.08, 0.29] of two steps (+2.5 px), more than the backward path (gap +6.6 px [4.9, 8.2]) or a norm-matched random direction (+2.5 px [1.2, 3.8]), but off-target change (6.1 px across, +0.93 m/s) is as large as the on-target one. The chord gives the same numbers as the spline (gap 0.00 px). The real reference shows why: the same clip's context window two tubelets later advances the forecast by 1.15 [1.05, 1.24] of two steps (15.5 px), yet leaves the context time readout unchanged (+0.01 steps), because the re-encoded window gets token positions 0-3 again. The time code tracks token position inside the window, not where the disk is on its path, and the edit itself mostly washes out downstream (the final-point time readout moves +0.14 / +0.34 steps for a +2 edit at points 12 / 22). Reversing the time path (t -> 3 - t) does not reverse the forecast (heading change 14 deg at point 12, 38 deg at point 22; 0% / 7% flipped), while really reversing the context frames flips it (161 deg, 99% of clips). So time is decodable and steerable at the layer, but it is not a fourth physics variable the predictor uses: forecast timing comes from the disk's content, and the per-step code behaves like a frame-index (positional) code amplified by training.",
 "slide_numbers": {
  "title": "The time-step code is steerable but the predictor ignores it",
  "real_window_+2_advance_fraction": "1.15 [1.05, 1.24] (15.5 px); context time readout +0.01 steps",
  "spline_+2_point22_advance_fraction": "-0.21 [-0.42, -0.01]; vs backwards path gap +0.02 px [-1.97, 1.84]; across 14.1 px; speed +1.97 m/s",
  "spline_+2_point12_advance_fraction": "0.19 [0.08, 0.29]; vs backwards +6.55 px [4.87, 8.16]; vs random +2.54 px [1.16, 3.84]; across 6.1 px",
  "reversal": "time-path reversal: heading change 14 deg (pt 12) / 38 deg (pt 22), 0% / 7% flipped; real reversed frames 161 deg, 99% flipped",
  "positional_confound": "shared time direction: pairwise cos 0.73 trained vs 0.05 untrained (pt 12); shared-curve share 0.77 vs 0.007; |cos| to speed axis 0.015 (null 0.072)",
